            <ArticleTitle>Primary Mediastinal Large B-cell Lymphoma Exhibiting Endobronchial Involvement.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3147-3150</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Primary mediastinal large B-cell lymphoma (PMLBCL) is one of the subtypes of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. We experienced a rare case of PMLBCL that exhibited endobronchial involvement. A 33-year-old Japanese female with the chief complaints of epigastralgia, back pain, and nausea visited a primary care hospital. Computed tomography of the chest and abdomen demonstrated a bulky mass in the left anterior mediastinum, multiple pulmonary nodules, axillary lymph node swelling, and a pancreatic tumor. Fiberoptic bronchoscopy showed a white-tinged irregularly shaped endobronchial tumor accompanied by capillary vessel dilation in the left upper lobar bronchus. Taken together, these findings resulted in a diagnosis of PMLBCL.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
